   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public authorities law, in relation to transportation\nauthorities",
    "basePrintNo": "S815",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S815-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§1299-a, 1299-c, 1299-bb, 1299-dd, 1301, 1303, 1326 & 1328, Pub Auth L (as proposed in S.3959-B & A.7822-C)",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S815\n\nSPONSOR: KENNEDY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public authorities law, in relation to transporta-\ntion authorities\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nAmends a 2022 chapter relating to the appointment of a transit dependent\nrepresentative on various transportation authorities.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSections 1 through 8 amends Chapter 801 of the Laws of 2022, which\nrequires the Niagara Frontier Transit Authority (NFTA), Rochester-Gene-\nsee Regional Transportation Authority (RGRTA), Capital District Trans-\nportation Authority (CDTA), and Central New York Regional Transportation\nAuthority (Centro) to have at least one voting member who is an individ-\nual who is limited to public transit as their primary transportation\nmode because of a disability.\n\nThe chapter amendment modifies the definition of \"transit dependent\"\nmembers to clarify that voting members must be transit dependent due to\na permanent disability, and also permits any local or statewide transit\nadvocacy organization to make recommendations of transit dependent indi-\nviduals for the Governor's consideration when making her appointments.\n\nSection 9 is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nTransit dependent individuals throughout upstate New York have recently\nwitnessed a reduction in routes serviced and an increase in fees, given\nthe tight fiscal climate and overall economic stresses of the past\nseveral years. Key decisions regarding re-routing, termination of\ncertain bus and train lines, and rate increases have been made and it is\nimportant that when contemplating these issues, regional transportation\nauthority's hear the voices of those most directly affected.\n\nThis legislation requires that the NFTA, RGRTA, CDTA, and Centro have at\nleast one of their voting members be an individual who relies on public\ntransit as their main mode of transportation because the individual has\na permanent or temporary disability. This individual would be appointed\nby the Governor; local or statewide transit advocacy organizations may\nmake recommendations of transit dependent individuals to be considered\nfor appointment.  Public transit systems are very important to a wide\nvariety of communities across the state, who rely upon them for access\nto services, healthcare, and employment, affordable means of transporta-\ntion, and the efficient movement of people throughout regions. These\ntransit dependent individuals' needs are unique to each region, and\nincluding them in the decision-making process would inform each regional\ntransportation authority of the specific needs and challenges faced by a\nregion.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2022: Underlying bill (S.3959-3) passed unanimously. Chapter 801\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect in the same manner as the underlying chapter.\n",
